# Test profile for WORKSPACE_HOST_PATH/WORKSPACE_USER (docker-compose.yml,
# .env.example) - exercises the auto-detect path: WORKSPACE_USER is left
# unset here, so `nix run .#up` should fill it in with your own `id -u`:
# `id -g` because WORKSPACE_HOST_PATH is set below. See
# scripts/validate-workspace-bind-mount.sh, which checks this.
#
# SECURITY WARNING - read .env.example's own WORKSPACE_HOST_PATH comment
# before pointing this at anything but a disposable scratch directory:
# it's a bind mount into the one container that runs agent-issued shell
# commands unreviewed, so the agent gets real read/write access to
# whatever host path you set here. This profile defaults to
# .bind-mount-scratch/auto specifically because that's throwaway - if you
# copy this as a starting point for a real profile, don't reuse a
# directory you care about.
#
# Usage (from the repo root):
#
#   cp scripts/test-profiles/bind-mount-auto.conf.example ./bind-mount-auto.conf
#   mkdir -p .bind-mount-scratch/auto
#   # fill in ANTHROPIC_API_KEY below (blank is fine if you're only running
#   # validate-workspace-bind-mount.sh, not a full phase-*.sh script)
#   nix run .#up -- ./bind-mount-auto.conf
#   sh scripts/validate-workspace-bind-mount.sh ./bind-mount-auto.conf
#   nix run .#down -- ./bind-mount-auto.conf
#
# The `mkdir -p` before `up` matters: if WORKSPACE_HOST_PATH doesn't
# already exist when the stack comes up, Docker creates it itself -
# usually root-owned - which defeats the point of auto-detecting your own
# uid:gid, since the directory would need root to write to it either way.
# Create it yourself first so it's already owned by you.
#
# ./bind-mount-auto.conf (once copied) is gitignored, same as .env - this
# .example template is the only copy meant to be committed.

ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

WORKSPACE_HOST_PATH=./.bind-mount-scratch/auto
WORKSPACE_REPO_PATH=/repo
# Left unset on purpose - this profile is specifically testing the
# auto-default path.
WORKSPACE_USER=
